{
  "assessment": "ISSUES_FOUND",
  "summary": "The script has a critical correctness issue: it always exits 0 regardless of migration status, which breaks the stated contract and makes the wrapper unsuitable for CI pipeline composition.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "file": "scripts/check-migrations.sh",
      "line": 10,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "comment": "The script unconditionally exits 0 on line 10, contradicting its own contract stated in the comment (lines 2–3): 'ALWAYS exit 0 and report the outcome as JSON — callers compose on .status. A nonzero exit is reserved for harness failures (bugs in this script), never for check failures.' This is correct in principle, but the implementation is incomplete: if `npx prisma migrate status` fails (line 7), the script still outputs JSON and exits 0, which means callers cannot distinguish between a harness failure (e.g., prisma not installed) and a migration check failure. The current logic treats all nonzero exits from prisma as 'failed' status, but a missing prisma binary or permission error should be a harness failure (nonzero exit). The script needs to differentiate: (1) prisma command not found or permission denied → exit nonzero; (2) prisma found but migrations pending → exit 0 with status=failed; (3) prisma found and migrations ok → exit 0 with status=ok.",
      "suggestion": "Capture the exit code and stderr separately to distinguish harness failures from check failures:\n\nset -uo pipefail\nstatus=failed\noutput=$(npx prisma migrate status --skip-generate 2>&1)\nexit_code=$?\nif [ $exit_code -eq 0 ]; then\n  status=ok\nelif [ $exit_code -eq 1 ]; then\n  # prisma exited 1 for pending migrations (check failure)\n  status=failed\nelse\n  # exit code > 1 indicates harness failure (command not found, etc.)\n  exit $exit_code\nfi\nprintf '{\"status\":\"%s\"}\\n' \"$status\"\nexit 0"
    }
  ],
  "good": [
    "The script correctly uses `set -uo pipefail` to catch errors early.",
    "JSON output format is well-formed and matches the stated contract.",
    "The wrapper pattern (always exit 0 for check outcomes, nonzero for harness failures) is sound in principle and appropriate for pipeline composition."
  ]
}
